 to get started in talking to  folks a little bit about their career  paths so  as you think about how you ended up  being a professor and researcher  studying the things you study how did  you get there what did you say when you  were a kid that what you wanted to be  when you grew up and how did that evolve  well i had a very different career path  than most people in education my  undergraduate major in college was  actually physics and electrical  engineering  but i graduated from college with my  degree in that area to discover  that i have no talent in that field at  all  it was a terrible disappointment to my  father who is an engineer  and i was at a party one night  which i i did a lot at that time uh  talking to a friend who told me about a  teaching job he'd been looking for a  teaching job and it was in private  school middle school where they needed a  seventh and eighth grade math teacher  would also coach  and this just happened to be the third  week of august  so  because my father  always claimed that the only reason i  was hired is i passed a mirror test they  put here my nose had fogged and so i got  the job  but i started teaching middle school and  i just fell in love with it i know a lot  of people struggle with that age of  children but i i just thought they were  terrific  so as a contingency of me being hard i  had to gain my certification so i began  taking education classes then i gained  my certification  and then after several years decided  that i really wanted to go back to  graduate school to learn how to become a  better teacher so i began in in boston  and then ended up at the university of  chicago where my chair my advisor and  the chair of my doctoral dissertation  committee was benjamin bloom  so it was from that  that i really became interested in the  whole area of of educational  measurements evaluation uh learned about  the taxonomy learn about instruction and  mastery learning and then began working  with chicago public schools did that for  several years and  decided after that i really wanted to go  into university position so  i accepted a position here at the  university of kentucky in the department  of educational  school and counseling psychology  and where i spent most of my academic  career  fantastic  so as i alluded to you have written a  lot of books and articles and  impressive library so if you had to pick  out some of the the areas that you think  that you say hey  i have a lot to to say about this topic  what are some of those areas that you  feel like are  important to that that you have some  important things to talk about  right  well my very first book was on  implementing mastery learning  and this was an extension of what i had  learned with benjamin bloom he was the  person to develop the whole concept of  mastery learning  and then when i began working with  chicago public schools we were leading a  team of of people there really to  implement bloom's ideas into the public  school system in chicago  so that was really my first interest the  instructional programs and the idea of  of using assessments as learning tools  but my my dissertation was actually in  the area of change and teacher change in  particular  so that led me into a profound interest  in none of the change process but  teacher learning and professional  learning experiences for teachers  so uh developed a book developed an  evaluation model for  professional learning looking at how we  can translate professional learning for  educators into  learning for students  um  then that  from that  got me interested in sort of using  assessments as learning tools as we had  through mastery learning that took in  the area of assessment  and then when we gather that information  we have to report it and communicate it  to parents and to families and that  brought me into theory of grading and  that's really what i've been focusing on  for the last several years of grading  issues and how to really enhance that  understanding what we know about the  grading process and then how we can  improve it to  enhance the communication between  schools and homes  that totally makes sense and i see how  things built on each other to get to  where you are today so before we get to  the grading piece i'd love to talk a  little bit more about mastery learning  since it is also at khan academy a  foundational uh piece in terms of how we  think about it so for those who are  watching who don't know um dr benjamin  bloom is considered one of the pioneers  and then dr gusky has taken many of  those lessons and applied them and  shared them with the world as well  how do you when you're when you went in  and were first talking to teachers or  even now when you're talking to teachers  who are just learning about mastery  learning how do you introduce it to them  in terms of what are the main the key  points and the key elements of it  well i always introduce mastery learn to  teachers as  a way to solve the two biggest problems  the teachers face  uh the first being the whole notion of  motivation you know how do i how do i  motivate students to learn things that  they may not consider importing their  lives or  don't seem as as relevant  and the idea is that the key to  motivation and what we know  seems to be the foundation of building  motivation is success i mean students  persist in activities which they're  successful and they  avoid with passion those things in which  they're not successful or believe they  cannot be  i mean i always  use the example of video games  i mean the biggest selling video game of  all time is pac-man  i mean nothing has ever outsold pac-man  it's a  like a 17-18 billion dollar uh industry  and the purpose of pac-man is basically  to gobble up dots  now  what makes that interesting what makes  that relevant to anybody's life nothing  and then some people say well maybe it's  behavioral maybe it's based on the  rewards you get  but that's not true  i mean